<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-siddiq" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-siddiq.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">June 632 · Medina</p><h1 class="sp-title">Abu Bakr on Becoming Caliph</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-siddiq">Abu Bakr</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>When the Prophet Muhammad died in Medina in June 632, the question of who should lead his community arose at once. The Medinan Helpers met in the hall of the Banu Sa'ida to choose a leader of their own, and Abu Bakr and Umar hurried to join them; after a heated debate Umar pledged his allegiance to Abu Bakr, the Prophet's closest companion and father-in-law, and the others followed. In the mosque Abu Bakr then addressed the people as the Prophet's successor.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Now I am Ruler over you, albeit not the best amongst you. If I do well, support me; if ill, then set me right.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">The early Arabic histories, from Ibn Ishaq's life of the Prophet on, record the speech; here in William Muir's translation, The Caliphate: Its Rise, Decline, and Fall (1891).</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“Now I am Ruler over you, albeit not the best amongst you. If I do well, support me; if ill, then set me right.” — Abu Bakr, Abu Bakr on Becoming Caliph" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-abu-bakr-on-becoming-caliph.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec sp-ft" id="full-text"><h2>The full speech</h2><div class="sp-ft-body"><p>&quot;Oh people! Now I am Ruler over you, albeit not the best amongst you. If I do well, support me; if ill, then set me right. Follow the True, wherein is faithfulness; eschew the False, wherein is treachery. The weaker amongst you shall be as the stronger with me, until that I shall have redressed his wrong; and the stronger shall be as the weaker until, if the Lord will, I shall have taken from him that which he hath wrested. Leave not off to fight in the ways of the Lord; whosoever leaveth off, him verily shall the Lord abase. Obey me as I obey the Lord and his Prophet; wherein I disobey, obey me not. Now rise to your prayer, and God have mercy upon you!&quot;</p></div><p class="sp-ft-credit">Text: <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Caliphate:_Its_Rise,_Decline,_and_Fall/Chapter_1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>, tr. William Muir (1891). The text is in the public domain; the Wikisource transcription is shared under CC BY-SA 4.0.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Abu Bakr ruled for only two years. He put down the revolts of the Arab tribes that broke away after the Prophet's death, the Ridda wars, and sent the first armies into Iraq and Syria. Ali, the Prophet's cousin and son-in-law, held back his own pledge for months, and the question of who should have succeeded Muhammad has divided Sunni and Shia Muslims ever since.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda wars</a><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a></div></div></section>
